{"title":"2024 CEMC TUC Problems/Relay B Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems/Relay_B_Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are fewer than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> students in Mr. Patel’s class. He can arrange the students in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> equal-sized groups. He can also arrange the students in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> equal-sized groups. How many students are in Mr. Patel’s class?\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems/Relay_B_Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/2/df290664cf90881dec7ee8d27f6da0910eb8f1fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"17\" /> and the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/3/a832b76c4f4a4ec13ac1b557da4fed6006e64d00.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 \\times 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />. From these prime factorizations, we see that the least common multiple of the two is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/e/c7e96847fbcccaa7900aa61abae9ef6d4f6f3fe2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2^{2} \\times 3 = 12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"92\" height=\"18\" />. This means that the number of students in his class must be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/f/edf074831eb5bc9e61d6d6e09f525a86e3068f6a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>However, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/c/8acbe9120ee49aea0cfd78c26c7ede1f1997e10d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12 \\times 2 = 24 &gt; 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"136\" height=\"15\" />, while <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/2/a52fdd8f9e81ce5555e17b109954bc8e668f9dea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12 \\times 1 = 12 &lt; 20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"136\" height=\"15\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/5/c85b9964821036604b1ba7154912a5c7cd8a9d10.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$12 \\times 2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" /> goes over <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/3/f8366cd6196dd8a9da6d38a3e9eafb109e99d53e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, the class must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/6/38693a75a9e00c962455566cda9d06d94da1738b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{12}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"30\" height=\"23\" /> students.\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC\" title=\"2024 CEMC TUC\">2024 CEMC TUC</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2024 CEMC TUC Problems (page does not exist)\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2024 CEMC TUC Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems/Relay_A_Problem_4\" title=\"2024 CEMC TUC Problems/Relay A Problem 4\">Relay A Problem 4</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems/Relay_B_Problem_2\" title=\"2024 CEMC TUC Problems/Relay B Problem 2\">Relay B Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2024 CEMC TUC Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2024 CEMC TUC Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2024 CEMC TUC Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems/Problem_4&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2024 CEMC TUC Problems/Problem 4 (page does not exist)\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2024_CEMC_TUC_Problems